@missplainjane3905  I have learned to speak a decent amount of Korean and can use it when needed, especially at banks, hospitals, traditional restaurants, and in smaller towns.

But most busy parts of Seoul--Hongdae, Sinchon, Jongro, Itaewon, Gangnam, Jamsil, etc.--will have English-speaking staff, English menus, signs, and so on. So you could get by in those places pretty well just speaking English.

That said, learning Korean has minimized communication problems and has given me more job opportunities, so I don't regret learning it.

@kaikimhyde There are a lot of bizarre parallels between Korea and Ireland, and Japan and UK.

UK and Japan are both island nations with an active monarchy that had past colonial ambitions, and a history of feudal systems (knights/samurais with castles). Plus they drive on the right-hand side of the vehicle, are stereotyped for their bad teeth, are known for their reserved nature and polite manners, yet very indirect communication skills. Ironically, they also love to drink tea, make rock music, have an interesting sense of humor, very quirky fashion scene, and their national dish is apparently a type of curry.

Ireland and Korea, on the other hand, are the smaller, yet more extroverted, drunk and aggressive neighbor to the west. They tend to be easier to talk to, yet also more blunt and short tempered than Japanese and Brits, and their drinking culture is more intense. A lot of them are also overzealously religious (Catholic Irish and Protestant Koreans (so many cults in Korea)). They historically hold resentment to the British and Japanese for occupying their land, and committing horrible atrocities to their people (Irish potato famine, and Korean comfort women). Unfortunately, their liberation came at a heavy price of having their land split into two (North Korea + South Korea, and Republic of Ireland + Northern Ireland).

@noidsuper I appreciate your respectful response; however, I disagree with all of your statements. This response demonstrates a profound ignorance of apartheid, its objectives, & outcomes, as well as the current state of affairs in South Africa. I’ve lived through both systems with full awareness, & have done a fair amount of research & speaking to people about their experiences.

S.A. politics, like all politics around the world, is far more nuanced than what a study of mainstream media would lead one to believe. I feel the sharing of personal experiences is important in that we all are intuitive & can make distinctions between truth & the tide of unavoidable propaganda we get exposed to. The main reason why history repeats itself is because people don’t invest enough time in understanding what really happened.

For the sake of easy reading for anyone interested, I’ve posted some information pertaining to this response about S.A. & the apartheid government. All of these details can be researched fairly easily online. Anyone who wishes to engage in a further civil exchange is most welcome.

